.TH SUDO 1 "September, 2026"

.SH NAME
\fBsudo\fP \- elevate privileges

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBsudo\fP \fIcmd\fP [\fIargs\fP]...
.br
\fBsudo\fP \fB\-s\fP [\fIcmd\fP]...

.SH DESCRIPTION
Prompt the user to authenticate, and, if they are permitted, execute a command using elevated privileges.
.PP
This implementation is very naive and should not be expected to offer any real security.
.PP
A cache is maintained of users who have recently authenticated successfully so that they do not have to enter their password again.
This cache does not differentiate between sessions.

.SS Options

.TP
.B \-s [\fIcommand...\fP]
Run the configured \fB$SHELL\fP once privileges are successfully elevated.
If \fIcommand\fP is provided, each argument will be escaped and joined with spaces to form a single argument for the shell's \fB\-c\fP option.

.SH SPECIAL FILES
\fBsudo\fP stores its configuration of users who are permitted to use it in \fB/etc/sudoers\fP.
Configuration is limited to specifying the usernames of those users.
No other options are supported as of writing.
.PP
The cache of users who have authenticated is maintained in the directory \fB/var/sudoers/\fP.
Cached authentication is restricted to a particular session ID.

.SH NOTES
This implementation of \fBsudo\fP provides a callback\-driven interface so that it can be extended with different frontends.
One such frontend is
.IR gsudo (1),
which provides a graphical interface.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gsudo (1),
.BR libtoaru_auth (3)

